{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>From twenty years old and upward, etc.</b> If this count was for the sake of the indwelling of the Shekhinah, why did it say ״כָּל יוֹצֵא צָבָא״ — what does 'going forth to army' have to do with the indwelling of the Shekhinah? And according to what I explained above, that the need for this count was to know whether there were twenty-two thousand for each camp of the Shekhinah out of the twenty-eight camps, so that they would be a likeness of the hosts on high — then it speaks well: ״כָּל יוֹצֵא צָבָא״, for he is fit to be as one of the host of the Height, as is found in the Midrash (Shemot Rabbah 15:6) that Israel are called 'hosts,' as it says (Exodus 12:41): ״יָצְאוּ כָּל צִבְאוֹת ה׳״, just as the angels are called 'hosts,' as it says (Isaiah 37:16): ״ה׳ צְבָאוֹת אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל יוֹשֵׁב הַכְּרוּבִים״." "And according to its plain sense, 'going forth to army' means for war. For had they not sinned with the spies, they would have entered the land immediately. And even though sometimes there is a valiant man under twenty years of age, nevertheless YHWH does not desire the might of man, but the matter depends on merit — and every twenty-year-old, by virtue of being subject to punishment in the heavenly court, is then all the more scrupulous in his deeds, and presumably he has more merits then that will plead on his behalf in battle. For after all, the priest proclaims: 'Who is the man who is afraid' — because of transgressions in his hand, 'let him go and return to his house'; and anyone under twenty presumably is not so scrupulous in his deeds and does not have so many merits to outweigh his transgressions."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:56.728844375Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}